WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.400
I mean,
the first barber I went to

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.360
was a white English guy

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.600
and I got butchered.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.680
I was absolutely butchered

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.280
to the point where,
on Monday,

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.040
when I went
into primary school,

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.600
I didn't want to
take my cap off.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:24.840
Of course not.
Of course not.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:25.760
And, when I took it off,

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.920
the laughter was worse than
it is here.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.520
-That's the worst, isn't it?
-It was horrible.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:32.760
About as quiet as a mouse?

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.040
Download Tinder.
Download Hinge.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.360
Trust me.
Your girl is out there, fam.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:47.880
Gus, how long
have you been married, mate?

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.480
I've been married
for 39 years.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.320
I'm still together
with my wife.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:53.920
Raised by my grandmother.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.960
She gave as much love
as she could give

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.800
and I have that
to be grateful for.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.600
I went on Tinder
to chat to girls, alright?

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.360
And it just so happened that

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.440
I chatted to the girl
that became my girl.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.080
I went to a party
in Thornton Heath

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:11.520
with a mate of mine.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.760
I saw her and it was weird.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.160
I just knew straight away
that

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.360
I would marry her.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:19.560
I knew that straight away.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.240
I started chatting with her

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.240
and she gave me
a bogus telephone number.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.600
I was gutted.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.000
I was totally
and utterly crestfallen.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.840
But there was a party
at the same house

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.160
three months later.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.320
I finessed things
a lot better than I did

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.680
the first time around

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.520
and we arranged to
meet again afterwards.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:42.880
And we've been together
ever since.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.120
I think, for me, the
most important part of that,

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:50.040
in all honesty,

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.760
was to be able to transfer
that onto our children.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.400
We don't realise
how much we model

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.680
what we want
our relationship

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.880
based on either
our parents' relationships

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.320
or significant relationships
that are in our,

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.640
you know, in our family
or in our network.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.480
We take a lot of that in
subconsciously.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.280
For me, having been raised

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.440
predominantly
by my grandmother,

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.000
traditional,
typical, Jamaican…

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.520
Everything she did was
for the three of us.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.480
To be a woman
in her mid-60s,

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.760
to take on
three grandchildren,

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.560
raising them
while your daughter

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.000
has absolved herself of
responsibility.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.960
She is and always will be,
you know,

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.720
my role model, my hero.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:37.520
Some people walk in

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.200
and then they go straight
into the chair!

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:40.680
I've been here
for an hour and a half.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:45.120
My dad used to cut my hair
when I was a little boy.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.320
But the funny thing is

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.520
my dad has been bald
for so many years

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.840
and,
when he used to cut my hair,

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.960
he forgot that he needed to
comb out the knots.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.200
He'd have the razor
just going…

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.640
And I was just like: "Ah!"

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.040
He'd be like:
"I didn't know."

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.960
Growing up, my parents were
very affectionate.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.560
You know, my mum was always
a very loving person.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.280
She loved to
get people together.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:12.080
She always wanted to see
other people happy.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:13.960
She was always sort of

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.160
sacrificing
her own happiness

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:17.640
for
other people's happiness.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.640
A lot of that
has rubbed off on me.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.840
Especially after
she passed away,

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.080
I feel like I've kind of
embodied her in some way.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.680
I sort of took on that role
in a way because

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:32.800
I wanted to feel like
mum was still…

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.080
That she's present,

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.400
spiritually
or in whatever way.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.840
There's something
in our society

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:43.560
which has definitely been
rooted in my upbringing

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:45.960
which basically suggests
that, as men,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.360
we should suck it up,
you know?

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:49.840
We should just deal with it
internally.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.960
We don't need to be
cathartic.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.400
We don't need to talk
and communicate.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.360
And that really stymies us.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.000
I guess,
also from my perspective,

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.560
just reflecting,
it just holds us back from

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.840
having the space to actually
not only receive love,

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.120
but also as well show love
and care

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:06.560
when people are going
through challenges,

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.080
when people put up
the flags.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:14.000
Coming out
of a relationship,

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:15.720
I was with her
for 16 years in total.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:16.680
And, since we split up,

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:19.560
I've been exploring,
trying or striving to be

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:23.560
more vulnerable with my
emotions and sharing more.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.040
Because I really do see that

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:27.720
that was one of the reasons,
the big reasons,

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.720
why I've gone through
my divorce.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.160
And maybe
I put too much of that on

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.200
her telling me how she felt,

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.320
but she probably couldn't
tell me how she truly felt

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.040
because I hadn't created
a safe environment

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:39.280
for her to do just that.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.880
I don't know where the need
to be stoic is rooted

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.160
or where it's coming from,

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.000
but I know that it's passed
down from my dad.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.080
You know, he's old school.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.360
He's, you know, never
gonna talk to you about

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.400
what he's feeling.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.160
But I remember,
at one stage,

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:53.960
he turned around and said:
"Carl, look."

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:55.280
"I think I'm depressed."

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.000
And I squirm at
my response to that

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:01.680
because,
when I look back at that,

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.520
my response was: "What do
you mean you're depressed?"

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:05.720
"Stop being silly. Come on.
Man up." You know?

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.080
And I see that almost as
a cry for help.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.480
And, yet still,
my response was

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.720
one that he instilled in me

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.240
of being
just ridiculously closed.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:18.800
Yeah, I just think that

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:20.560
if I was probably
a bit more open to

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:21.760
recognising the signs

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.720
and recognising
what he was going through,

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:26.200
then, yeah,

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.040
I could have been a bit more
supportive to him.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:32.040
I've been
a very angry young man.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:33.960
I was definitely going down
the wrong path.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:37.960
I used to worry because
I had so many girlfriends

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:39.240
that I just didn't think

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:41.280
I was ever going to meet
someone

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:42.760
who I could live with

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.480
or who could tolerate me,
you know?

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:47.320
But then it happened.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.440
You know, one or two people
have said about, you know,

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:50.840
creating space.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:53.200
I created space to allow
someone to come in

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.720
and now be that person that

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.800
I wanted and looked for,
you know?

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:00.280
There's so much luck
in these situations.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.400
I moved to London in 2000.

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:08.040
In 2001, I met Hazel

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:11.080
and my instinct just told me
that

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:13.040
there was
something about her

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.080
so, while I didn't know

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:17.560
where it was going to
end up,

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.880
you know,
I followed my instinct.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:23.440
We got married in 2008.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:27.000
Had our first son in 2009.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:33.040
Baby number two, Mr. Isaac,
was due end of March 2013.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:35.120
I took her to the hospital.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:36.920
At seven o'clock
in the evening,

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.320
she gave birth to Isaac.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:41.200
But by

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.800
two o'clock
the following morning,

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.680
so just about five hours
later, she died.

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:56.920
And I wasn't supposed to be
a single dad

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.440
with a three-year-old
and a newborn baby

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.920
having to tell
Aaron at three that

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.320
his mum had died
and she wasn't coming back.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.160
He spoke about
what it was about Hazel…

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:16.520
Yeah.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:20.600
I saw my grandmother in her.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:24.200
That's it.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:28.400
So I've lost the two women
the closest to me

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:31.120
in the world,

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:34.080
but I'm still here.

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:46.120
It's interesting, isn't it?

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:48.000
Because we do have
the barber shop,

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:50.280
but we don't talk about
those types of things.

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:51.320
We have the rum shop,

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:53.280
but we don't talk about
those types of things.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:54.400
You know, we go to…

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:55.320
There's a betting shop,

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.200
but we don't talk about
those types of things.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:00.880
But we do meet a lot,
you know, as black men.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:02.280
We don't have
those discussions.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:03.640
I don't think I had that,
you know.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:05.440
You know
how we're kind of fed

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:06.960
this definition of
masculinity as well?

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:07.920
Yeah.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:09.800
I wasn't that definition of
masculinity.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:12.240
Barber shops,

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:15.440
I would say are
a safe space for most,

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.360
but definitely from my peers

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.400
that are in the LGBTQ+,

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:25.880
it can be challenging to
find a safe space.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:29.280
Even myself, my confidence
in barber shops.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.840
It was always a case of:

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:33.760
ok, go in,
get your hair cut, leave.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:35.720
So…

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:37.680
It's generally a safe space,

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:39.360
but what about the people
on the fringes of it?

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:41.320
I think James Baldwin talks
about

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.320
something as
a state of abjection

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:47.120
and that's to feel as a
foreigner in your own home.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:54.920
You know,
a lot of times we're taught

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.520
or the definition of
black masculinity

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:57.600
has been sold to us

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.560
as some sort of
hyper-masculine

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:03.120
kind of structure
or construct.

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:05.320
I fully get you on that.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:06.320
When you talk about tribes,

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:07.440
it's actually
really important.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:09.040
I'm lucky enough to have,
like,

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:10.800
a sick, sick group of mates.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:12.600
We've got this group
called Feelings

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:14.760
and that's separate to
a number of other groups

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:15.680
that we're all in.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:17.920
What we all try and do is
just support each other

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.760
and, whatever anybody's
going through,

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:22.680
we allow each other
that space

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:25.960
to be vulnerable,

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:28.480
to be your authentic self,
I feel.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.600
We're not all trying to do
the male thing

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:33.440
and be like:
"I'm a big man, yeah!"

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:35.000
"I'm nice.
Don't worry about me."

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:36.640
I think that takes
great strength

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:37.680
to be able to do that

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:39.040
and to be able to find it
in your heart

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:40.680
to be comfortable
in doing that.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:44.920
Men who say that, you know,
men shouldn't cry…

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.840
In a way,
I feel sorry for them

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:48.520
because sometimes I think:

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:49.680
"Well, what do you do, then,

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:51.840
when you're feeling like,
you know, crying?"

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:53.240
I'm not afraid to cry
with my friends.

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:55.640
I've seen the vast majority
of my friends cry,

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:57.360
especially my closest pals.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:09:59.440
This is what being masculine
is about.

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:02.200
I don't care what
their friends are doing.

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:03.720
I'm not doing what
their friends are saying

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:05.000
or not saying.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:07.720
I have to lead by example.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:10.640
But not just for my boys.

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:13.280
In terms of
my responsibility, too.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:14.520
In my community,

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.520
too many black boys,
too many,

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:23.640
too many black boys
going under.

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:26.280
There's a lot between these
ears, you know what I mean?

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:28.920
We just need the opportunity
to be able to speak about it

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:30.560
and really put ourselves
in positions that

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:33.520
we can have conversations
outside of things

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:37.080
that are just
physical prowess

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:40.200
or race relations topics.

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:41.320
There's so much more
out there

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:42.960
that we all wanna
talk about.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:44.800
We get pigeonholed into
these boxes

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:47.040
and I think
a lot of black masculinity

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:48.280
falls into that at times.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:56.400
For me and my experiences,

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.120
love really boils down to
respect.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:03.080
It is acceptance,

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:06.840
completely,
of another person or people,

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:10.240
regardless of their flaws
and their greatness.

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:15.760
I think, for me, love is…

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:18.160
It's like a commitment,
duty.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:19.840
It's responsibility that
you give

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:21.960
because
that is what you want.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:28.480
The way I am right now
in my life,

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:31.120
it's really about the boys

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:34.920
and me and them
as a family unit

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.520
and there being love shared
between us,

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:39.320
us looking out for
each other,

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.120
us having each other's back.

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:43.560
-That's what it's all about.
-I hear that.

